pr_reviewer_data.description:
Regarding #5061

I would like to offer an alternative to module augmentation and merging declaration more broadly.

We currently leverage module augmentation for decorators, that doesn't give any information about the level of encapsulation, worse, it does pollute the global type instances. I think this is bad, we can achieve that by trying to require all the decorators at registration-time.

We could export the decorators of different decorators from core plugins instead of just augmenting the global instance and request/reply:
```ts
export interface StaticReplyDecorators {
  sendFile(filename: string, rootPath?: string): FastifyReply;
  download(filepath: string, filename?: string): FastifyReply;
}

// Also usefull for Vanilla projects with fastifyPlugin
export const STATIC_REPLY_DECORATOR_NAMES = ['sendFile', 'download']
```

We could use this feature this way at plugin registration:
```
const { sendFile, download } = fastify.getDecorators<StaticReplyDecorators>(STATIC_REPLY_DECORATOR_NAMES)
```

More realist use case:
```ts
const { sendFile } = fastify.getDecorators<StaticReplyDecorators>('sendFile')
fastify.get('/', (request, reply) => {
  return sendFile.call(reply)
})
```

Yes, we have to leverage `call`, but it does work and doesn't break anything, there is just a bit of export to create, I can work on it.

Do you see any value in this feature?

